=========================================================
===		 GenMon Help        		      ===
=========================================================

             -- Key Reference --

Globals:
	F1:	Next window

	F2:	Run
	F3:	Stop/Break
	F4:	Run No Update
	F6:	Next Window
	F7:	Trace(Step Into)(+alt=src,+ctrl=asm)

	F8:  	Step Over  	(+alt=src,+ctrl=asm)
	F9:  	Enter Window Movement Mode
	F10: 	Activate Global Menu Bar
	Ctrl-F10: Activate local memu.

	alt-Z:  Zoom current window(if resizable)
	alt-P:  Print current window to log file
	alt-D:  Enter dos shell
	alt-Q:	Close Window
	alt-X:	exit program

Windows:
	alt-M:	Open Memory Window
	alt-R:	Open Register Window*
	alt-O:	Open Console Window*
	alt-E:	Open Expression Parser Window*
	alt-B:	Open BreakPoint Window*
	alt-S:	Open Symbol Window*
	alt-K:	Open Command Window*
	alt-T:	Open Text View Window
	alt-H:	Open This Help Window
	alt-W:  Open Watch Window
	alt-L:	Open source Level debug window*
	alt-I:	Open Info window(About)*
	alt-Y:  Open ASCII Chart

	* can only have one open at a time

String Requesters:
	alt-A:	Cut
	alt-C:	Copy
	alt-P:	Paste

	shift+left mouse click begins global copying,
	hold down and drag to the right to highlight
	text, then release, text will be copied to the
	clipboard(paste buffer).

Movement Mode:
	up,down,left,right moves window
	shift + up,down,left,right resizes window
	esc leaves movement mode

=========================================================
=========================================================
	     Window specific help:
=========================================================

Memory:
	The memory window is used to examine memory
	in a variety of formats, includeing bytes,
	words, long words, ASCII or dissasembled code.

	Keyboard shortcuts:
	    ctrl-h: Run to cursor(set break once, and run)
	    ctrl-m: Modify memory under cursor
	    ctrl-s: Set Breakpoint at cursor
	    ctrl-o: Set break Once at cursor
	    ctrl-n: Set break with count at cursor
	    ctrl-a: Clear all breakpoints
            ctrl-g: Set the dynamic running address.
            ctrl-b: Display hex bytes
            ctrl-w: Display hex words
            ctrl-l: Display hex long words
            ctrl-d: Display as disassembly
            ctrl-y: Display as ascii

=========================================================

Register:
	The register window is used to set and view the
	current	68000 registers. To change a register,
	simply click on the desired register, and an
	expression window will ask for the new value.

	The registers are shown in hex, and the
	flags are shown individually.

=========================================================

BreakPoint:
	The Breakpoint window is used to set and clear a
	variety of breakpoints. To set a breakpoint,
	choose set in the menu (or press ctrl-s) and
	enter the address at which you wish to set the
	breakpoint. To clear a breakpoint, position the
	cursor over the desired breakpoint and select
	clear from the menu(or press ctrl-c). Break Once
	creates a breakpoint which will clear itself
	after breaking once. Break with count will break
	count times, then clear itself.

	Keyboard shortcuts:

	    ctrl-s/Insert: Set breakpoint
	    ctrl-o: Set break Once
	    ctrl-n: Set break with count
	    ctrl-c/Delete: Clear break under cursor
	    ctrl-a: Clear all breakpoints

=========================================================

Console:
	The console window is a debugging tool used by
	your program as an easy way to communicate with
	the developer. By using print routines provided
	in the developer package, your program can send
	messages to the console window. 68000 exceptions
	are also printed here. There can only be one
	console window in GenMon.

=========================================================

Symbol:
	The Symbol window is used to create, delete and
	load symbols. To load symbols, choose
	"load symbols" in the menu(or press ctrl-l),
	then select the correct symbol file. To create
	a symbol, choose "Create Symbol" in the menu
	(or press ctrl-s) and enter the value you wish
	to set the symbol to. To delete a symbol,
	position the cursor over the desired symbol and
	select "Delete Symbol" from the menu(or press
	ctrl-c).

	Keyboard shortcuts:

	    ctrl-b: Set Breakpoint at symbol under cursor
	    ctrl-o: Set Break Once at symbol under cursor
	    ctrl-l: Load Symbol File
	    ctrl-s/Insert: Set Symbol(define new symbol)
	    ctrl-c/Delete: Clear symbol under cursor(delete symbol)
	    ctrl-a: Clear all symbols

	    (save symbols is not implemented, but does
	    anyone actually need it?)

=========================================================

Expression:
	The expression window is provided as a
	convenience for the developer. It provides a
	way for symbols and other expressions to be
	evaluated. It can be used a calculator or for
	resolving complicated expressions. Answers are
	given in hex, decimal, and binary. The first
	symbol(if any) matching the result is also
	displayed. Answers can be cut or copied to the
	cut/paste buffer and then pasted into onter
	input fields. There can be only one expression
	window in GenMon open at a time and it has
	no menus.

=========================================================

Command:
	The command window allows users to enter command
	line input to GenMon. GenMon's responses are
	also given in the command window. The command
	line has powerful scripting capabilities, more so
	than the keyboard macros. There can only be one
	command window open at a time.

	Keyboard shortcuts:
	    Up:   access previous command from history
	    Down: access previous command from history

=========================================================

Watch:
	The watch window is useful for keeping track of
	variables or expressions while the code is
	running. To set a watch, choose "Set Watch" in
	the menu (or press ctrl-s) and enter the
	expression you wish to watch. To clear a watch,
	position the cursor over the desired watch and
	select "Clear Watch" from the menu(or press
	ctrl-c).

	The expression will be updated every frame,
	displaying the result of the expression, a
	colon(':'), and then the original expression.

	Keyboard shortcuts:
	    ctrl-s/Insert: Set watch
	    ctrl-c/Delete: Clear watch under cursor
	    ctrl-a: Clear all watches

=========================================================

TextView:
	The text viewer allows you to examine the
	contents of any text file.

	up/down/left/right is used to position the
	cursor in the file(cursor coordinates are
	shown in the bottom right)

	pgup/pgdown moves up and down in the file
	by one page.

	ctrl-pgup/ctrl-pgdown moves to the top and bottom
	of the file.

	Keyboard shortcuts:
	    ctrl-l: Load text file
	    ctrl-f: Find text string in text file
	    ctrl-n: Find next occurance of text string
	    ctrl-g: Goto specified line in file

=========================================================

Help:
	The help window(your looking at it!) is a
	TextView window with a text file named
	'genmon.hlp' loaded into it. This file shows
	keyboard commands and some basic instructions
	in the usage of GenMon. It is meant to be an
	on-line reference. You may edit and add to
	this file with any text editor.
	(Also, check here and the readme for new commands
	that may not have made it into the manual).

=========================================================

Source:
	The source level debugging window allows you to
	see what file the code you are tracing was
	assembled from. To activate source level
	debuggging, you must load a .SLD file into
	this window.

	Keyboard shortcuts:
	    ctrl-l: Load .SLD file
	    ctrl-f: Find text string in current file
	    ctrl-n: Find next occurance of text string
	    ctrl-g: Goto specified line in file
	    ctrl-s: Set breakpoint at current line
	    ctrl-o: Set break once at current line
	    ctrl-c: Clear breakpoint at current line
	    ctrl-a: Clear all breakpoints
	    ctrl-h: Run to current line
	    ctrl-y: Goto given symbol

